 #:seon.task{:name "gate-sets-map-arity-gets-a-supplied-database"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(my.test/check {:seon.test/changed ['user/a-test]}) at a branch REPL refuses: seon.fn/gate-sets is called with [<db> {:seon.db/db <db> :seon.fn/seeds #{...}}] against its [:cat :seon.db/database-value [:sequential :seon.fn/sym]] arity, so a database is supplied in front of the map arity's own request (callers seon.test at test.clj:835 and :1066). Building that refusal then throws ClassCastException Keyword -> Map$Entry. Reproduced 2026-09-26 20:46Z on branch merge-cut1-opus."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/gate-sets seon.test/run}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/merge-cut1-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "caller-relint-follows-contract-consumers"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.fn/caller-files relints the direct callers of every function whose contract references a changed schema (transitively through :seon.schema/references) and of any :seon.fn/spec or arity-ref change, though publication lints with every type-derived linter off (seon.fn.analyzer/publication-config), so no contract can change a caller's publication findings. The a34078273 publication changed 4 schema keys: 57 consumers selected 241 files (the program) for a second clj-kondo pass, 10.7 s of a 15.2 s warm replay and most of a 47.6 s boot store->branch layer; true signature changes selected 3 files, all already reconciled. Patch: tmp/perf-boot-opus/fn-caller-files.patch (+26/-46). publication_cache_test's second change (spec only) then expects #{} instead of #{src/beta.clj}."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/caller-files seon.fn/index!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/a-three-file-changed-path-publication-takes-a-minute.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "report-identities-costs-a-second-per-call"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.fn/report-identities over one publication's 5,227 datoms took 1,312 ms per call on default (x2 = 2,623 ms; seon.db/with-declarations x15 2,669 ms, pull-many 1,300 ms), and publication, caller selection and adoption (source/changed-identities) each call it. Its work should follow the changed entities, read once per report."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/report-identities}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "fn-test-redefines-vars"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent "AGENTS forbids with-redefs over any Var, yet test/seon/fn_test.clj still redefines Vars in about ten passing tests (~169, 195, 885, 896, 925, 1463, 1503, 1577, 2019, 2031; with-redefs-fn ~1744, 3055). A redefinition is JVM-global: a concurrent member or agent calls the substitute. Replace each with an injected world or explicit argument value, as 238c868e0 did for the two red ones."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "build-artifact-is-a-second-indexing-path"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.fn/build-artifact is a second indexing path beside the production one. It analyzes with no cache root (seon.fn.analyzer/analyze turns the cache off outside the checkout, analyzer.clj:818-822) and takes a hand-written first-party list, while the production path seon.fn/program-entities and analyzed-artifacts (fn.clj:2641) read the checkout cache and derive first-party from the database's entities. It has no src caller. Its test callers (fn_test ~881, 2071, 2080, 2132, 2431, 2843; program_test 1348; reset_edges_test 188; adoption_entities_test 15; edit_test 261; test/runner_test 163) mostly pass an empty first-party list and carry the same latent 'no analysis of seon.db' refusal since a9449b7c3. Delete it and convert its callers to seon.fn/program-entities in the same commit."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/build-artifact seon.fn/program-entities}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "functions-without-tests-is-the-last-datalog-reach"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.fn/functions-without-tests has no caller outside fn_test and is the last reader of test-reach-rules and declared-reference-rules, a second statement of the declared-invocation relation beside seon.fn/declared-callers that can drift from it (111 s on default when measured 2026-09-17). Delete it with both rule sets, or derive coverage from call-reach. 2026-09-28: seon.health now calls it for the hot-and-untested warning (351-919 ms cold, retained by seon.health/derived per program version, 1 ms warm; 159 untested public functions); a replacement derived from call-reach must keep that caller."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/functions-without-tests seon.fn/declared-callers}
             :evidence #{"tmp/test-feedback-perf/proof.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "cross-file-derivation-inputs-rely-on-dependent-relint"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A file's derived entities also read other files' facts: the symbols its calls resolve to, a callee's host-bound flag, an invoked attribute's targets, and clj-kondo findings over another namespace's signatures. The per-file derivation key (seon.cluster/derivation-keys) holds none of them; the dependent and caller re-lint selection must re-derive those files when the facts change. Prove the selection covers each class with a regression, or add the facts' digests to the key."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/dependent-paths seon.cluster/derivation-keys}
             :evidence #{"tmp/boot-source-perf/evidence.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-armed-first-population-takes-a-hundred-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An armed first population of the whole program into a memory store (seon.fn/index! with the source manifest after accrete-schema-population!, as seon.fn-test/fresh-population-flattens-with-its-supplied-declarations does) took 102.5 s and admitted 13,372 operations (disposable root at 866eef6f5, 2026-09-27); a nuke's unarmed first population is about 128 s of its 136 s ready. cd769af69 removed one per-read whole-program derivation from the writer (an uncommitted value's builder identity); re-measure after it and profile the writer thread (JFR stackdepth 1024, tmp/boot-source-perf/evidence.md) before choosing a fix. Over the ten-second rule."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/index! seon.fn/reconcile-tx-in seon.db/carried-projection}
             :evidence #{"tmp/boot-source-perf/evidence.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "refused-publication-reads-have-no-redefinition-free-regression"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Two regressions refused a read by redefining seon.db Vars, and both seams moved: selection's declared-edge query became fn/declared-callers reading datoms (39d94eef0), so selection-test's injected refusal was deleted in red-triage r8; reconcile-tx-in reads through db/pull-many (296dede99), so fn-test refused-index-reads-never-become-publication-entities redefines a pull it no longer calls and answers transaction data. Prove refusal propagation with a genuinely refused read (a world whose read refuses), not a redefinition."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/index! seon.fn/tests-reaching}
             :evidence #{"test/seon/fn_test.clj" "test/seon/test/selection_test.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "contract-findings-has-no-scope"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The lint/contract entrance available to agents, seon.fn/contract-findings, is whole-program and answers 283k tokens. Done when a scoped entrance exists under my.* (a namespace or symbol set) that answers only the findings for that scope in a reply an agent can read, with a size regression."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/contract-findings}
             :evidence #{"tmp/repl-audit/change.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-edit-rewrites-every-definition-below-it"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A publication rewrites :seon.fn/form-span and :seon.program/analyzed-source-digest for every definition whose lines moved, not for the ones whose source changed: analyze-forms stamps each definition with its whole FILE's digest (:seon.fn.file/digest of the analyzed source) and same-indexed-entity? only skips a definition when everything but that digest is equal, so a moved span rewrites both. On default's history (2026-10-07, db-watch lane) one publish rewrote 2,674 form-span/digest datoms for 136 changed :seon.fn/source datoms (20x), another 2,892 for 327; overall 19.5k of the 162k history-only datoms on default are these two attributes. Each rewritten definition touches its aevt/avet leaves, so the files written follow the file length below the edit. Smallest fix: the file digest lives once on the file entity (:seon.fn.file/digest), not copied per definition; keep form-span but compare it as position, not as a change of the definition. Done when editing one defn in a 100-definition file writes O(changed definitions) program datoms (measured with watch.clj :since-basis), with a regression on that count."
             :must-read #{seon.fn/analyze-forms seon.fn/same-indexed-entity?}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/db-watch/findings.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "backticked-docstring-references-have-no-index",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "CLAUDE.md says a backticked fully qualified symbol or keyword in a docstring is a reference the index resolves into the subject's context, one resolving to nothing a problem on that var. No producer exists: seon.fn indexes :seon/tasks, :seon/must-read and :seon/see-also metadata (task-links) and analyzer :seon.fn/references, never docstring spans. Done when docstring backtick spans are indexed as reference edges with dangling ones reported, or the law is corrected.",
            :must-read #{seon.fn/task-links},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/context-design/reference-kinds-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "batched-analysis-uses-one-namespace-row-per-run"
  :kind :function
  :intent "seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch builds one ns form from the first row of a run of consecutive requests (partition-by on namespace name, fn.clj:1013), so later requests with a different row are analyzed under the first. Probe (JVM, default's database value): two requests in namespace probe.a, row 1 x -> clojure.string, row 2 x -> clojure.set, forms (x/upper-case \"a\") and (x/union #{1} #{2}). Batched: usages [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.string union] (the second is wrong); analyzed one by one: [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]. Correct: a run splits where the namespace row changes (not only the name). Proof: seon.fn-test with those two requests asserts [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-renamed-refer-drops-its-call-edge"
  :kind :function
  :intent "runtime-require-specs (fn.clj:814) emits :refer [target-name] only and drops the local name of a :rename, so a renamed refer is invisible to analysis. Probe (probe.alias7): (require '[clojure.set :refer [union] :rename {union upper-case}]) then (defn r2 [] (upper-case ...)) stores calls [defn] with no edge to clojure.set/union. Correct: the analysis ns form carries :rename so the call resolves to clojure.set/union. Proof: that pair of forms stores clojure.set/union in r2's calls."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/runtime-require-specs}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "private-flag-disagrees-with-the-var-for-computed-metadata"
  :kind :function
  :intent "(defn ^{:private (> 1 2)} falsy-meta [] 1) has Var :private false while the stored row says :seon.fn/private? true (fn.clj:746 reads clj-kondo's flag); the reverse holds for alter-meta! after the defn. Low severity. Correct: the row's private flag agrees with the Var's, or the source-derived privacy is stated as the rule. Proof: that form stores private? false."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/private-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-dependent-keeps-no-edge-to-a-callee-defined-after-it"
  :kind :function
  :intent "(defn bad1 [x] (nosuch-fn x)) then (defn dep1 [x] (bad1 x)), then in a later pass a working bad1; and (declare later) (defn early [] (later)) (defn later [] 1): early and dep1 keep calls [clojure.core/defn] and gain no edge when the callee is defined. Related: seon.turn.batch/a-declared-var-call-stores-no-edge (the declare half). Correct: a caller's edge to a callee appears once the callee has an entity, without re-evaluating the caller. Proof: the last table row of failed-def-design.md (probe 7)."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/failed-def-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "contract-facts-row-names-no-schema-in-its-refusal"
  :kind :function
  :intent "A defn whose contract names an unregistered schema refuses as \"derive: :malli.core/invalid-schema\" and its diagnostic never names :my.s5-03h/nosuch; the key is only in the cause chain (fn.clj:3151). Correct: the refusal carries Malli's humanized problems (malli.error/humanize) naming the missing schema key. Proof: that defn's diagnostic data contains :my.s5-03h/nosuch."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/contract-facts-row}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/failed-def-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "call-reach-seeks-once-per-changed-declaration"
  :kind :function
  :intent "seon.fn/call-reach seeks :avet once per seed for :seon.fn/calls, once per frontier name for :seon.test/subject, and seon.fn/declared-callers seeks once per requested value for each indexed declared-edge attribute (:seon.render/ai, :seon.render/html, :seon.render/form, :seon.schedule.task/function, :seon.effect/capability), although those attributes hold at most a few hundred datoms. A my.test/check whose seon.test/changes holds thousands of identities therefore makes seven reads per changed declaration: (seon.fn/gate-sets {:seon.db/db db :seon.fn/seeds <all 5,672 function symbols>}) on default at 953efe25d made 39,713 seon.db/datoms reads, 595,936 armed calls, 632 ms and 1.19 GB; root's one-test check after a restart counted 20,042 reads and 2,931 ms. Correct: each declared-edge attribute and the subject attribute is read once by a walk; only the caller seeks stay per target. Proof: seon.test-reaching-test/selection-reads-each-declared-edge-attribute-once."
  :must-read #{seon.fn/call-reach seon.fn/declared-callers}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/led-cache/reach-key-notes.md"}}]
